we're going to begin with the derek  shovin trial it has brought back a flood  of emotions for those watching all  around the country  and all around the world but arguably  nowhere more so than in minneapolis  itself the city where george floyd was  killed last may more than 10 months  after his death  we spoke to people in minneapolis to  find out how the trial is affecting them  as they watch it all unfold my name is  nakima levy armstrong  i'm a minneapolis-based civil rights  attorney and activist  i'm rt rabback ceo of the minneapolis  foundation and former mayor of  minneapolis  i am p.j hill i'm a community member  i'm a member of the church on 38th in  chicago i am the vice president of the  naacp  minneapolis chapter my name is sherry  albers  i live eight blocks from where george  floyd was killed  it's a daily gut punch i think for me  and  a lot of others in this community to see  the word minneapolis connected to  such a hideous crime it showed the video  again that was my first time watching it  in a long time i mean since it actually  happened  and man literally tears got in my eyes  and then literally  anger just at what we seem sometimes i  feel  traumatized from watching the witnesses  in particular  hearing the desperation in their voices  on those bystander videos is he  breathing right now  it's really sad and disheartening to  think about what they went through  and the burdens that they still carry  with them i didn't intend to watch the  trial  um i thought i had seen enough and i  turned it on on monday morning and i  couldn't stop  watching it it was too traumatic but i  felt  i needed to bear witness to what had  happened  our community is hurt we're healing and  now the trial is like ripping the  band-aid off  all over again  the trial is very traumatizing for many  of us in the african-american community  walking through every piece of this is  absolutely  excruciating but it's exactly what we  need to do  because the the the notion of  learning what is wrong of relearning  what was wrong as well  is playing out right now in front of our  eyes this was  the impetus for change i'm hopeful  that there's change i had good change  something inside all of us broke  you know what's going to happen this  this trial could really change the  landscape of things not only from  minneapolis  but the nation
 

